This peer gives application and abstraction directly in the universal domain and formalizes the sandwich calculation underlying the interpretation of typed abstraction and application.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`Uapply`).** Universal application decodes its first argument as a continuous self-map of \(U\) and evaluates that map at its second argument.

**Definition (`Ulam`).** Universal abstraction encodes a continuous self-map of \(U\), regarded as an element of the function-space domain, back into \(U\).

**Definition (`translateAbs`).** Given endomaps \(a\) and \(b\), the translation of a body \(f\) is the composite \(b\circ f\circ a\).

**Theorem (`Uapply_Ulam`).** Applying the universal abstraction of a map \(f\) to \(x\) gives \(f(x)\).

*Proof.* Decoding immediately after encoding is the identity on the function-space domain, so function-space evaluation reduces to evaluation of \(f\) at \(x\). ∎

**Theorem (`translateAbs_idMap`).** Translating the identity body between \(a\) and \(b\) gives the composite \(b\circ a\).

*Proof.* Composition with the identity does not change the intervening map. ∎

**Theorem (`example_826`).** For every neighbourhood system and endomaps \(a,b\), translating the identity body yields \(b\circ a\).

*Proof.* This is the preceding identity-body calculation, uniformly quantified over the neighbourhood system and the two maps. ∎

**Theorem (`translateAbs_sandwich`).** If \(a\) and \(b\) are retractions, then every translated body \(f'=b\circ f\circ a\) satisfies the sandwich equation \(b\circ f'\circ a=f'\).

*Proof.* Associativity gathers the two copies of \(a\) and the two copies of \(b\); idempotence of the retractions removes the repetitions. ∎

**Theorem (`translateApp_hasType`).** Suppose \(f\) satisfies \(b\circ f\circ a=f\). If \(x\) is fixed by \(a\), then \(f(x)\) is fixed by \(b\).

*Proof.* Evaluate the sandwich equation at \(x\) and use the assumption \(a(x)=x\). ∎
